says nothing about current product quality.\\n\\n**Discover by player workflow.** A maturity assignment covers an ordinary workflow and its\\nfailure/recovery edges, not just the happy path or a named function. Use the following coverage\\nto select area-sized repair work. Across a finishing campaign, cover every row; a single fix\\nchecks only the affected row and adjacent transitions. Reuse current results when the relevant\\nowners have not changed. These are assignments and completion bars, not new status IDs.\\n\\n| Player problem / coverage | Existing owner and concrete repair assignment | The result the player should get |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Start, stop and resume** | THE MACHINE + THE LONG GAME: cold New Game → Launch → control; save → title/restart → Continue; death → retry; failed/invalid load → recover. Cover Browser and Electron's shared route and any affected shell boundary. | No crash, endless spinner, invisible world, lost progress, duplicate rewards or dead end. A failed operation explains itself and leaves a usable route back. |\\n| **Controls and everyday play** | THE HAND + THE FIGHT: ordinary flight, targeting, mining, towing, fighting and camera/zoom; pause/settings/modal → resume; held/released inputs, rebinding and focus changes. | Input answers once, the camera and body agree, and closing a screen never leaves stuck controls, accidental actions or an unusable ship. |\\n| **Menus, HUD and text** | The workflow owner repairs functional UI within ORRERY: exercise title/new game/load/settings/pause, map, station/market/outfitting, jobs and Swarm draft/refit/results. Check narrow/default/wide layouts, long names/numbers, +40% pseudo-localized copy, populated/empty/error states, scrolling and focus. | No overflow, overlap, cropped meaning, off-screen action, unreadable feedback or dead control. Needed information and controls remain reachable by the supported input methods; motion/accessibility settings preserve meaning. |\\n| **The visible world loads and stays there** | THE PICTURE + THE MACHINE: camera approach/turn/zoom, fast travel, sector arrival/exit, combat spawns and save/load. Repair the actual asset resolution, fetch/decode/compose/compile/admission/residency owner that fails. | Ships, stations, rocks, wrecks and effects appear as authored bodies when needed; nothing usable is an empty lock, invisible collider, missing part or disappearing on-screen model. Loading failure is recoverable and cannot silently count as readiness. |\\n| **Off-screen work and loading pressure** | THE MACHINE with THE PICTURE/WORLD: bound predictive admission, decode/compile queues, draw submission, distant queries, texture/model residency and retirement. Check rapid direction reversals, arrivals and repeated sector revisits. Separate render cost from simulation authority. | Nearby and imminent content wins priority; distant content does not flood expensive queues or remain resident without need. Culling/residency does not pop visible bodies, discard imminent arrivals, erase NPC consequences or change default visual quality. |\\n| **Hitches and long-session stability** | THE MACHINE: repair identified main-thread stalls, allocation/GC churn, repeated setup, unbounded scans, resource/event/timer leaks and stale async callbacks through dock/undock/jump/fight/save/load cycles. | Stable frame pacing and bounded resources over ordinary transitions. Use existing demo/min-spec budgets; report frame tails and missing-body evidence as well as averages. A smoother empty scene does not pass. |\\n| **Transactions, jobs and progression** | THE WORLD + THE LONG GAME: accept → do → settle/abandon/fail → reload; trade, buy/fit/use, cargo custody, credits, law and unlocks. Include invalid/stale selections and repeated clicks. | Displayed price, eligibility, inventory, physical result and saved result agree. Settlement happens once; cancellation/failure leaves coherent play and useful feedback. |\\n| **Visual and audio finish** | THE PICTURE + THE EAR + the affected workflow owner: broken transforms/materials/lighting, clipping/","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"C:\\Users\\93rob\\.claude\\projects\\C--Users-93rob-Documents-GitHub-SpaceFace\\c020a9ae-f380-4499-9216-2daa8d6e2392\\tool-results\\bkj2mxq8t.txt","persistedOutputSize":74955},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01N1GQT6UVrvk3xYEj4osRGo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"classifier"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BKXAtKpButd2Mq5ytKQMrt","type":"tool_result","content":"## 6. The demo defect ledger — everything a demo player will hit (live, opened 2026-09-22)\n\nOwner, 2026-09-22: *\"total-fix mode … if you see bugs somewhere that aren't related that would hurt\nthe demo experience fix them … documented or fixed issues when you find them rather than letting it\ngo by.\"* This table is the ONE place a noticed-but-not-yours defect goes; root `AGENTS.md` §7 is\nthe law, this is the ledger. Any defect a demo player would meet qualifies — screen, gameplay,\nperformance, audio, save, flow — not just pixels.\n\n- **Add** one row when a defect is real but you are not fixing it this session: what a stranger\n  sees, where (bench shot id, route, or system), the demo step it hurts, and the cheapest known\n  repro. If you cannot write a repro, log what you did see and how.\n- **Remove** the row in the same commit that fixes it (screen rows: re-shot through the bench\n  first). Rows are never struck through, marked done, or moved to an archive — the ledger holds\n  only open defects, so its length is the honest count of what a player will hit.\n- **Burn it down:** any sitting may claim any row, and a sitting dispatched into files carrying an\n  open row handles that row as part of its unit. If the table grows past one screen, clearing rows\n  outranks new queue units.\n- **Owner ruling 2026-10-04 — the consumer law:** clean-machine captures, soaks and perf\n  measurements are consolidated in [`QUIET_HOST_PROGRAM.md`](./QUIET_HOST_PROGRAM.md); a capture\n  exists only to close a row, validate a landed fix, or serve as the before/after of an\n  optimization made in the same sitting. No row holds open on host weather (\"needs quiet host\")\n  or recurrence luck as its close condition — such a row closes with its evidence instead\n  (D48/D85/D111/D131 closed on this ruling 2026-10-04). Freeze evidence is collected only during\n  the program's general playtest block (QH-7); no sitting is dispatched to reproduce a historic\n  freeze at its reported place.\n\nThe screen rows below were each seen on a real screen (`node scripts/ui-bench.mjs --shot=<id>`,\nreal DOM over a still). Rows marked **bench** may be fixture-only; the owner of the row proves\nwhich before closing it.\n\n| # | Where (shot id / route / system) | What a stranger sees | Demo step | State |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| D188 | Physical-phone startup and renderer recovery | Production `7ac10849f2` is READY on Vercel. Its frozen source passes public New Game, touch controls, rotation, manual Save, reload and Continue in iPhone14-profile WebKit with zero JS/WebGL/critical HTTP errors. Required uploads no longer use optional budgets; mounted pod packages retain residency; cancelled first-picture work cannot publish; failures retain the original cause and Retry/Back. | Phone New Game / Continue; rotation; interrupted startup | OPEN for physical-handset compatibility: iPhone14 Chrome has not been directly exercised. Windows WebKit profile is functional browser evidence, not handset/GPU timing acceptance. Earlier isolated8a failed after Save at reload; published7ac passes that route. Evidence: `.devshots/mobile-deployment/oct16-published-mobile/report.json`, frozen digest `ed6ff8adaaa66b1d630a0cb01c6cdb43271af4cefcfa87efc9d749398c1aba57`; PR436 superseded by published master. |\n| D191 | Ceres refinery pocket population; `test/ceres-table-authority-census.test.mjs`, `test/ceres-active-pockets.test.mjs` | Same-sector re-entry payload churn is fixed by `0e71b4baf0`; `d062a50e6b` removes 35 temporary arrays and 20 Sets per tick without changing traffic population. Current seed 14920 CPU attribution (111 live entities, 240 warm/120 sample ticks) places physics 4.42 ms, tacticalAI 2.13 ms and FlightV3 1.19 ms/tick; these busy-host inclusive values route repairs, not FPS acceptance. A separate physics phase sample places 3.07/4.11 ms in the native solve/guard step without isolating safely removable work. | Enter Ceres and fly the refinery pocket | OPEN for overall simulation performance. Traffic normalization passes 45 focused checks including deterministic Ceres behavior and save/Continue; authored population stays intact. Next work needs a causal owner within the dominant solve/guard or AI phases; repeating the same top-level census adds no material evidence. See MATURITY_2026_10_15.md. 2026-10-17 orch-demo-m30: real-body attribution names Rapier `stepWithEvents` dominant (2566 ms inclusive over a 30 s weave, 170 live entities) with tactical-AI `_sensorFrameFor → entityContacts` next; a sensor-loop hoist candidate was tried and REJECTED (entityContacts sampled 164.6 → 257.7 ms; .devshots/bm5-bm6/). The dominant cost is the native solve step itself — the open question is now scoped to it. OPEN. |\n| D199 | Asteroid/background contrast and player-truth ownership | Three native ownership diagnostics measured no submitted-rock contrast. The atomic attempt has 17 bitmap timeouts and one JPEG whose asteroid pixels remain unknown because a vertex-deformed painted stellar-light draw may cover them. Radius-square contrast is diagnostic. The unsuccessful capture candidate is preserved as a patch and withdrawn from source. | New game, starter seam; Swarm Ricochet Foundry seed4242 | OPEN. No brightness or threshold change is justified. Do not repeat the unchanged diagnostic or grow the capture system during the current performance campaign. Evidence: MATURITY_2026_10_15.md, .devshots/oct16-d199-swarm-atomic/, and .devshots/oct16-d199-withdrawn.patch. |\n| D226 | `assets/ships/release/parts/places/place_landmark_wreck_cathedral.glb` (found during the SFQ-I071 LOD silhouette sweep, 2026-10-18) | The wreck-cathedral landmark's in-file LOD1 drops ~176 small LOD0 shells outright — the ring spires (~1.1×7.7×7.8 WU) and greeble plates carry zero LOD1 vertices — so the spire fringe thins the moment its LOD1 toggles in | Fly past the wreck-cathedral landmark at medium-far distance | OPEN. Cause known: the GLB's authored LOD1 simply omits those shells; fix is a Forge LOD1 re-author of that one file. Not a ship: all 147 release parts with in-file LOD0+LOD1 were scanned (meshopt decode, connected-component coverage vs LOD1 + always-visible verts) and every ship hull, whole-ship body, engine and accessory part keeps every silhouette shell at LOD1. Repro: rerun that coverage scan on the file. |\n| D198 | New Game screen open; `src/ui/screens/newGame.js` (`onShow`, `setLaunching`), startup census/compile scheduling in `src/render/startupGpuResidency.js` | Historical Menu → New Game capture froze the picture for about a second: 1.2 s of main-thread work with 250 ms stalls; current residual cost is unverified. | Menu → New Game | OPEN. The rect-loop allegation is repaired: `f3bcb16f8e` caches hull-ring geometry and reuses nodes, `7b2d9c9675` batches yard measurements before writes, skips unchanged builds, rests the position watch and defers name focus until after paint; `088c183cca` preserves 0.01 px layout signatures. Current yard/ring fixtures pass 40/40, including unchanged-trigger coalescing, resize invalidation and zero layout reads during spins. Historical remaining measurements — 245 ms GL command-buffer waits, 1104 microtask checkpoints (104 ms), and 138 ms forced style/layout under `ui/kit/dom.js` button activation — do not establish current costs or a missing paint yield. The current candidate also schedules embark decode requests through the existing post-present arm, preserving coalescing and bounded fallback while cancelling hidden/retired owners; six focused regressions pass. Its effect on the historical spike remains unmeasured, so D198 stays open pending current-route attribution. 2026-10-17 orch-demo-m30: current-route attribution done; two legs landed (hidden-screen prewarm style/layout 83 ms → 0 ms; a 3.06 s covered-title warm-up draw now waits until the title is requested again; evidence `.devshots/bm2-d198/RESULT.md`). Remaining: the visible-title shader warm-up and the final click cost, measured at 190 ms under 82 % host CPU contention (campaign workers were running) — attribution continues on a quiet host. OPEN. |\n\n\n| D130 | Sustained flight and frame pacing | The whole-game 3x hitch target remains unmet. Current frozen 3eed versus 337b: Adventure early 36.5→34.3 submissions/s and 35→28 actual pose gaps over 50 ms; settled 35.3→38.1/s and 50→30 gaps (p99 65.0 ms). No missing eligible player meshes or stale-sim samples; early canvas content remains UNKNOWN after one decoder error. Swarm improved substantially in the prior eight-cell comparison, but this is not whole-game acceptance. | Ordinary flight; station interactions | OPEN: identify the current Adventure main-thread/GPU owner, optimize it without reducing authored quality, then directly compare. Other applications remain running; report contention rather than inventing a quiet-host result. Evidence: MATURITY_2026_10_15.md and .devshots/performance-campaign/oct15-shadow-hud/. 2026-10-17 orch-demo-m30: one Adventure owner leg LANDED — `cameraRoofAt → clearanceNeighborhoodRaw` pruned by conservative world-space upper-Y (exact heights preserved; dense-raster test visits under 1% of triangles): clearance query 1.648 → 0.139 ms/frame, `cameraRoofAt` inclusive 3157 → 422 ms, p99 130.9 → 73.1 ms, submissions/s 27.5 → 38.8 under recorded host contention (.devshots/bm5-bm6/). Row stays open for the whole-game 3x target. OPEN. 2026-10-18 real route on the owner laptop (Core Ultra 7 155U, Intel integrated graphics, headed Chromium, browser/Devin/other apps running, so contended): New Game reaches flight and renders; `probe-frame-solid --cpu-profile` adventure seed 4242: frame p50 48 ms, p95 98 ms, p99 145 ms, 46% of frames over 50 ms, longest 3.6 s; 102 draw calls and 50 program switches per frame; self CPU is (program) 24%, three.js 25%, renderer.js 5%, vfx.js 3%. Draw-ca